Dear Jordan,

Following the kick-off, here is where we stand on the building.

Floor slab
- The building dates from 1994. The landlord has no as-built drawings of the floor
  slab and no record of its thickness or reinforcement.
- The current tenant is in the building until 15 January 2027, so no core samples
  or load tests can be taken before then.
- If the slab turns out to be too thin in places for the high-bay racking loads,
  there will be extra work: local strengthening, different anchors or a changed
  rack layout. We cannot tell you which, or how much, until the cores are taken.
  Nobody can. Please include an allowance for it in the price.

Fire protection
- A high-bay storage system needs in-rack sprinklers, and the local fire authority
  has to approve the design.
- The authority only reviews a final design. They may accept the standard layout,
  or ask for extra sprinkler levels, flue spaces or changes to the rack layout. We
  have no way of knowing in advance, and neither does our fire consultant.
- So any redesign effort after their review is an open risk we'd expect you to
  allow for.

Still ours to decide
- Power: we haven't decided whether our own electrical contractor brings the
  three-phase supply to your drop positions, or whether you include that in your
  scope. We'll decide once we see your power requirements.
- Site access: access for your installation team depends on when the landlord hands
  the building over. We're still negotiating that date with the landlord and will
  confirm it.
- Mezzanine: we haven't decided whether to keep the existing mezzanine over docks 1
  to 3 for the packing area or have it removed. Our operations and facilities teams
  will decide and let you know.

Kind regards,
Grace Okoye
